<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alaouite dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1631">1631</a>–present</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Alawi dynasty, Alaouite dynasty of Morocco</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alaouite dynasty is the ruling house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, a sharifian family claiming descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> through his grandson Hasan. Settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tafilalt">Tafilalt</a> oasis since the 13th century, it rose to power from the 1630s, and Mawlay al-Rashid reunited the country in the 1660s after the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saadi-dynasty">Saadi dynasty</a>. It has reigned ever since, through the French and Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a>, to King Mohammed VI.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alaouite-dynasty.jpg" alt="Alaouite dynasty" width="320" height="328" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Government of Morocco · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Morocco.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Almohads ruled the Maghreb and al-Andalus in the eleventh and twelfth centuries; the Saadi sultans destroyed the Portuguese Aviz dynasty at the Battle of Alcácer Quibir in 1578 and crushed the Songhay Empire in 1591; and the <b>Alawi dynasty</b>, which seized power in 1631, reigns to this day.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Government was centred on the makhzen, the royal household and administration, supported by tribes that supplied soldiers in return for tax exemptions, a system later inherited by the <b>Alaouites</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Alaouites</b> were a sharifian family settled in the Tafilalt oasis.</blockquote><a href="/state/alaouite-sultanate">Alaouite Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Moroccan unity was restored under the <b>Alaouites</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/dilaiyya">Dila'iyya</a></li></ul></section>
